from register_common import add

add(56,'Picard group of a projective bundle',[97,98,99,100,101],3871,4080,'proof sketched','B',
'For a finite locally free E of rank at least2 over S, every line bundle on P(E) is uniquely p* M tensor O(n), with n a locally constant integer function on S. The relative Picard sheaf is the constant Z sheaf. On rank1 strata P(E)=S, so this extra Z factor is absent.',
'EGA III1 Cor4.6.5 and Remark4.6.6(i), printed142, explicitly announce this classification for chapterV and supply the deformation step. Stacks0BXJ gives the field/UFD computation and0B9S the base-change mechanism. This is now standard, but the unpublished draft is not mistaken for a section of published EGA IV.',
'EGAIII1,Stacks0BXJ,Stacks0B9S,Stacks01OA','Proof: on every field fiber Pic(P^(r−1))=Z, so choose its degree n. Proper cohomology/base change, H^1(P^(r−1),O)=0 and evaluation give an open neighborhood where L(−n)=p*M, with M=p*L(−n). These local degrees glue as a locally constant function; p*O=O proves uniqueness of M with the comparison. Finite-presentation approximation reduces a general base to the noetherian case.',group='picard')
add(57,'Finite degree partition corrected in the next witness',[97,98,99],3871,4000,'corrected by himself later','B',
'Scan97 gives a finite partition of S by degrees of a line bundle on P(E); scan98 indexes the partition by all integers. A finite partition is automatic only when S is quasi-compact. Uniqueness is with a chosen comparison isomorphism, not absence of scalar automorphisms of a line bundle.',
'The locally constant-degree statement in C-SA23-hyp-056 is the correct unrestricted version. Example S=the disjoint union of Spec(k) indexed by Z and L|P^1_n=O(n) uses every degree, so the finite assertion cannot be retained.',
'EGAIII1,Stacks0BXJ','Proof: the indicated disconnected base is a scheme, E=O² is finite locally free and the componentwise line bundles glue because the components are open and closed. Infinitely many nonempty degree strata occur.',group='picard')
add(58,'Graded rank-one projective lemma',[100,101],4004,4080,'open on the pages','E',
'The proof asks for a standard source or proof that a graded rank-one projective module over a positively graded polynomial algebra over a field is graded free, up to a degree shift; it uses this to compute Pic(P^r).',
'The polynomial ring is a UFD, so its rank-one projectives are free; the graded refinement follows by choosing a homogeneous generator, or graded Nakayama after localization at the irrelevant maximal ideal. Stacks0BXJ supplies a direct Čech proof of the required projective-space Picard group, avoiding the missing citation.',
'Stacks0BXJ','Proof: a finite graded projective module M has M/(x0,…,xr)M concentrated in one degree when rank1. Lift a homogeneous basis element. Graded Nakayama makes the map A(−n)→M surjective; its projective kernel has rank0 and is zero over the domain. Hence the map is an isomorphism.',group='picard')
add(59,'Degree-n maps and linear automorphisms',[102,103,104,105],4086,4230,'proof sketched','B',
'For finite locally free E,E′ over S with rank(E)≥2, an S-map P(E)→P(E′) on a stratum of constant relative degree n≥0 is represented by a base line bundle M and a map E′→M tensor Sym^n E whose evaluation generates p*M(n), modulo compatible isomorphism of M. Degree0 maps factor through S; degree1 maps arise from surjections E′→M tensor E and are linear closed immersions. Automorphisms form PGL(E).',
'The universal quotient property of Stacks01OA proves the classification. The automorphism scheme is the invertible determinant open in the projective bundle of End(E), hence affine of finite presentation locally on S; its scalar quotient is PGL(E). The source asks for justification of this representability, supplied here.',
'Stacks01OA','Proof: pull back the universal quotient line bundle, use C-SA23-hyp-056, and adjunction p*O(n)=Sym^n E. For n=1 generation is equivalent to surjectivity E′→M tensor E. For automorphisms both maps have degree1 and give inverse linear maps up to scalar. D_+(det) in Proj Sym(End(E)^vee) is affine and represents invertible matrices modulo Gm.',group='picard')
add(60,'Relative divisors and the corrected symmetric-power dual',[5,106,107],4235,4310,'corrected by himself later','B',
'For E finite locally free of rank at least2 everywhere, the relative effective degree-n divisors in P(E), n≥0, are represented by P((Sym^n E)^vee). Scan107 expressly corrects scan5, which used Sym^n(E^vee). A divisor is a fiberwise regular section of p*M(n), equivalently a sub-line-bundle M^−1 of Sym^n E. The separate cancelled restatement on scan112 omits the dual on Sym^n E and does not repeat the rank-at-least2 hypothesis; it is retained as another source occurrence, not silently normalized. The formula is not extended to rank1: then P(E)=S, the relative Cartier divisor is empty, and the degree labels do not distinguish different divisors.',
'The relative Cartier criterion and universal quotient property establish the corrected formula. Over arbitrary characteristic, dual of Sym^n E must not be replaced canonically by Sym^n(E^vee); divided-power issues make the distinction real. Degree0 parametrizes the empty divisor.',
'Stacks056P,Stacks01OA','Proof: every nonzero homogeneous polynomial over a field is a nonzerodivisor in its polynomial ring. Fiberwise nonzero sections therefore give relative Cartier divisors. A locally split line inclusion in Sym^n E dualizes to a rank-one quotient of (Sym^n E)^vee, exactly the functor represented by the stated projective bundle.',group='divisors')
add(61,'Multiprojective line bundles and the omitted base factor',[108,109],4314,4390,'false as stated','B',
'Theorem2.5 gives Pic(product P(Ei))=Pic(S) plus one locally constant degree for each factor, with ranks≥2; Proposition2.6 then writes only the product of O(ni), omitting the pullback of a base line bundle.',
'Iterating the projective-bundle theorem proves the former and exposes the omission in the latter. Multiprojective divisors of degree(ni) are P((tensor_i Sym^ni Ei)^vee), ni≥0. A classification without the base factor is false whenever Pic(S) is nonzero.',
'EGAIII1,Stacks01OA,Stacks0BXJ','Proof: take S=P^1_k, all Ei trivial rank2, and L pulled back from O_S(1). Every fiber degree is zero but L is nontrivial, since restriction to a section recovers O_S(1). Thus the printed formula cannot hold.',
refine='Include p*M in the multiprojective line-bundle formula; with this correction the degrees and M are uniquely determined with comparison. The corresponding relative divisor scheme is the projectivization of the dual tensor of symmetric powers.',group='divisors')
add(62,'The general projective parameter for relative divisors',[110,111],4396,4474,'proof sketched','B',
'Let f:X→S be proper, flat and finitely presented, with universally f*O_X=O_S, and L invertible. If a finitely presented Q corepresents G↦f*(L tensor f*G), the divisors with associated bundle L up to a base twist form a retrocompact open in P(Q). For geometrically integral fibers this is all of P(Q).',
'The cohomology corepresentability ingredient is in his own published EGA III2 Th7.7.6, printed69, under a locally noetherian base, proper f and coherent base-flat sheaf;7.7.9(i) gives base change. The relative Cartier and projective-quotient constructions give the open divisor functor. The universal f*O condition is needed for the scalar quotient and uniqueness, and geometric integrality is needed for every nonzero section to be regular. The whole parameter statement is not attributed verbatim to7.7.6.',
'EGAIII2,Stacks056P,Stacks01OA','Proof: a quotient Q→M corresponds to a section of L tensor f*M. The locus where its restriction to every fiber is regular is open by the flat finite-presentation Cartier criterion; properness of X/S gives the required relative open condition in the parameter. On geometrically integral fibers every nonzero section of an invertible sheaf is regular.',group='divisors')
add(63,'Fixed points and transposition of divisor families',[112,113,114,115],4479,4629,'proof sketched','B',
'For D⊂X×_S T a relative Cartier family, its fixed-point locus Z consists of x such that the whole fiber T_x lies set-theoretically in D_x. Under universal openness of T/S, Z is closed. If T/S is flat, locally finitely presented with geometrically irreducible S1 fibers, D transposes to a relative Cartier family over X exactly away from Z. With T proper, the analogous associated-point obstruction Z′ is closed.',
'The openness and fiberwise Cartier arguments are standard consequences of the relative Cartier criterion. The S1 and geometric irreducibility assumptions cannot be replaced by topological irreducibility alone; regularity is tested at all associated points. The source explicitly distinguishes Z from Z′ in the general case.',
'Stacks056P','Proof: X−Z is the image of (X×T)−D under the open projection, hence open. An irreducible noetherian S1 geometric fiber has its unique generic point as its only associated point. A local equation is regular exactly when it avoids that prime, equivalently when it is not nilpotent; mere nonzero is insufficient on a nonreduced fiber. The relative Cartier locus W in X×T is open under the stated finite-presentation and flatness hypotheses. For proper T/S, the image of its closed complement under X×T→X is closed, giving Z′.',group='linear_systems')
add(64,'Basepoint-free linear systems and nondegeneracy',[116,117],4634,4708,'proof sketched','B',
'A basepoint-free linear system on X/S with projective parameter P corresponds to a morphism to the dual projective bundle whose image on every geometric associated component spans that bundle. This componentwise condition ensures every member is a relative Cartier divisor.',
'The universal quotient construction proves the map correspondence; the componentwise spanning condition is exactly nonvanishing at all associated points. The weaker condition that the union of all components spans does not suffice, since a hyperplane may contain one component.',
'Stacks01OA,Stacks056P','Proof: a section of an invertible sheaf is regular iff it avoids all associated points. A nonzero parameter hyperplane pulls back to zero at an associated generic point precisely when it contains the corresponding component image. Applying this on every geometric fiber gives the assertion.',group='linear_systems')
add(65,'A rational map does not recover fixed divisors',[117,118,119,120,121,122],4673,4914,'corrected by himself later','B',
'The draft first tries to recover a linear system from its rational projective map, then restricts to depth≥2 along the omitted locus and restores the nondegeneracy condition on scan122. Fixed divisors cannot be recovered from the rational map alone.',
'Hartogs extension supplies uniqueness under the depth hypothesis. The unrestricted statement fails already for a one-member system: different effective fixed divisors give the same rational map to P^0. The later version is therefore a genuine correction, not just stylistic rewriting.',
'EGAIV2,Stacks0E9I,Stacks01OA','Proof: on P^1_k take the one-dimensional section spaces generated by sections with divisors{0} and{∞}. Both rational maps have target P^0 and agree on their common domain, while their divisors differ. Removing a codimension1 base locus destroys the Hartogs uniqueness needed for recovery.',group='hartogs')
add(66,'Relative Hartogs extension and invertible extensions',[118,119,120,121,122],4713,4914,'proof sketched','A',
'Let X→S be locally of finite presentation, F a quasi-coherent finitely presented S-flat module, T⊂X closed, and j:X−T→X a quasi-compact open immersion. Fiberwise depth of F at every point of T at least1 gives F→j*F|U injective; depth at least2 gives an isomorphism. In the line-bundle application X/S is flat with fiberwise depth of O_X at least2 on T: then a bundle L on U extends invertibly exactly when j*L is invertible, uniquely with its comparison.',
'EGA IV2 5.10.2 and5.10.5, printed114–116, proves the depth1/2 Hartogs criteria over locally noetherian schemes;6.3.1, printed138–139, compares flat-module depth with fiber depth. EGA IV3 11.3.7–8, printed135–136, supplies the finite-presentation relative regular-sequence mechanism used to remove noetherian hypotheses. The rational-map application still requires componentwise nondegeneracy.',
'EGAIV2,EGAIV3,Stacks0E9I','Proof: locally a finitely generated ideal defines T because j is quasi-compact. The fiber-depth hypothesis supplies a length1 or2 regular sequence in that ideal after localization; EGA IV3 11.3.8 lifts its regularity for the base-flat module. The local-cohomology sequence0→H^0_T(F)→F→j*F|U→H^1_T(F)→0 then gives injectivity or bijectivity. In the noetherian case this is exactly IV2 5.10.2/5.10.5. Apply the depth2 statement to Hom bundles for uniqueness of invertible extensions; if j*L is invertible it supplies existence.',group='hartogs')
add(67,'Factoriality and extension of Cartier divisors',[121,122],4835,4914,'proof sketched','E',
'In the locally factorial setting, a divisor on an open whose complement has codimension at least2 extends uniquely as a Cartier divisor; the associated line bundle extension gives the corresponding rational-map/linear-system extension when the stated nondegeneracy remains true.',
'The regular-local factoriality theorem and the Cartier–Weil correspondence are classical ingredients. The source does not prove an unrestricted extension across codimension1, and the earlier fixed-divisor ambiguity must not be reintroduced.',
'EGAIV2,Stacks0BXJ','Proof: on a locally factorial noetherian scheme a codimension1 cycle defines a Cartier divisor. Closure of the codimension1 components from U gives the unique Weil divisor on X because the complement contributes none. Local factoriality makes this closure Cartier.',group='hartogs')
add(68,'Every nonzero section, not every section, must be regular',[123,124,125],4920,5035,'false as stated','B',
'The section criterion on scans123–125 twice says every geometric section is regular, omitting the word nonzero. On a nonempty fiber the zero section is a zerodivisor, so the literal statement excludes all systems.',
'With nonzero restored, the criterion is exactly the componentwise regularity statement in C-SA23-hyp-064. For geometrically integral fibers it is equivalent to injectivity E(s)→H^0(X_s,L_s). This is a reading alarm, not a decision about the ink.',
'Stacks056P,Stacks01OA','Proof: multiplication by0 on a nonzero local ring has nonzero kernel. On an integral fiber any nonzero section of a line bundle is regular; hence injectivity of the parameter space is necessary and sufficient.',
refine='Require every nonzero geometric parameter section to be regular. Under geometric integrality, require the map from the fiber parameter vector space into H^0 of the line bundle to be injective.',group='linear_systems')
add(69,'Grassmannian classification and complete systems',[125,126],4999,5083,'proof sketched','B',
'For proper flat geometrically integral fibers and a cohomology corepresentative Q, linear systems with fixed parameter rank correspond to locally free quotients Q→E^vee, modulo parameter isomorphism, hence to a Grassmannian. A system is complete exactly when its fiber spaces equal all H^0(X_s,L_s).',
'EGA III2 Th7.7.6 supplies Q; the Grassmannian quotient functor gives the moduli statement. Completeness is preserved by base change when stated geometrically with the required cohomology/base-change isomorphisms. A marginal apparent negation in scan126 conflicts with that formulation and is flagged without alteration.',
'EGAIII2,Stacks089R,Stacks0B9S','Proof: adjunction identifies a section map E→f*L with a map Q→E^vee; fiberwise injectivity of the former is fiberwise surjectivity of the latter. Nakayama gives a locally free quotient. Completeness means this quotient has full fiber dimension and the cohomology base-change comparison is an isomorphism.',group='linear_systems')
add(70,'Faithful flatness does not make a divisor family monomorphic',[126,127],5041,5124,'corrected by himself later','B',
'Deleted5.3 claims a faithfully flat locally finitely presented X/S makes every linear-system family a monomorphism into Div. Revised4.3 adds a geometric associated component whose global functions equal the residue field, a condition automatic in the proper integral case under discussion.',
'The deleted claim fails for X=P^1_k−{a,b}: the two parameters a,b of the hyperplane family both restrict to the empty divisor. The revised global-function condition controls units and makes proportional sections the only coincidence. The weaker faithfulness of the projective automorphism action is distinct.',
'Stacks01OA,Stacks056P','Proof: X→Spec(k) is faithfully flat and finitely presented. Restrict the universal degree1 divisors from P^1; members a and b are both empty on X, so the parameter map is not injective on k-points and hence not a monomorphism. On a component with H^0(O)=k, equal divisors differ by a scalar unit, giving the required proportionality.',group='faithfulness')
add(71,'Projective-frame rigidity',[128,129],5129,5200,'proof sketched','E',
'The projective-frame lemma requires each specified component image to span the projective parameter space. Under that hypothesis an automorphism fixing the images is scalar on the associated vector space; merely spanning by the union of components is insufficient.',
'This is the classical projective-frame argument. It supports the revised automorphism-faithfulness proof, with scheme-valued infinitesimal verification still needed for a monomorphism of functors.',
'Stacks01OA','Proof: n+2 points in projective general position form a projective frame; fixing the first n+1 diagonalizes a representing matrix, and fixing their sum makes all diagonal entries equal. A union of coordinate points spans but admits a diagonal torus of automorphisms, so the weakened union formulation fails.',group='faithfulness')
add(72,'Infinitesimal faithfulness and tangent formulas',[129,130,131],5168,5279,'proof sketched','B',
'For an effective Cartier D⊂X the tangent space to Div at D is H^0(D,N_D/X); the tangent space to Hom(X,P) at f is Hom(f*Ω_P,O_X). The proof of functorial faithfulness needs Artin-local/infinitesimal control, not only injectivity on geometric points.',
'Grothendieck’s published Bourbaki Exposé221 (May1961),5.3–5.5, printed270–271, explicitly gives the Hilbert tangent and Cartier-divisor formulas. The square-zero calculation also proves the Hom formula. The text’s O_P in that formula is a type error. Its references to future §§25–26 do not establish the general arbitrary-functor monomorphism criterion, which remains a separate incomplete step; the1961 tangent result is already known when this draft is written.',
'GrothHilbert1961,Stacks01OA,Stacks056P','Proof: deform an ideal locally generated by t to(t+εa); changing the generator identifies a modulo(t), giving Hom(I/I²,O_D)=N_D/X. A map deformation is a derivation f^*O_P→O_X, equivalently Hom(f*Ω_P,O_X). These are first-order statements only; induction along square-zero extensions is required for the stated stronger functor conclusion.',group='faithfulness')
add(73,'Brauer–Severi parameter spaces by descent',[132,137],5284,5320,'stated without proof','B',
'Uniqueness of the parameter identification permits fppf descent of a linear system, but the descended parameter need only be a Brauer–Severi variety. Scan137 proposes treating projective bundles and their twisted forms using PGL torsors.',
'This is the standard PGL-torsor/Brauer–Severi correspondence. Reineke–Schröer2017 §2, printed453–454, explicitly recalls the general scheme version and the GL-lift obstruction; only those preliminaries are used. The proposed future exposition is not present in the supplied pages.',
'Stacks01OA,ReinekeSchroer2017','Proof: choose local identifications with P^(r−1). Their overlaps give a PGL_r cocycle; effective faithfully flat descent yields the twisted projective scheme. Lifting the cocycle to GL_r is exactly the additional obstruction to obtaining the projectivization of a global vector bundle.',group='brauer')
add(74,'Recovering a system from rational members',[132,133],5284,5355,'open on the pages','C',
'Proposition4.4 asks for a proof that, under H^0(X,O_X)=k, the set of k-rational members determines a linear system, and asks for the exact generality over imperfect fields and counterexamples. The hypotheses about regularity and available rational members must be retained.',
'For the geometrically integral proper setting and actual k-linear section subspaces, equal divisors identify sections up to k* and equality of all projective k-points determines the subspace. The broader imperfect-field formulation on the page was not matched to an exact theorem; no counterexample violating H^0(O)=k is used.',
'EGAIII2,Stacks01OA','Proof in the stated restricted case: two sections with the same effective divisor have ratio an everywhere invertible global function, hence an element of k*. Every nonzero vector in a k-vector space defines a k-rational projective point. Equality of their represented lines therefore recovers the subspace. This does not prove the extra imperfect-field/descent assertions.',confidence='medium',group='linear_system_questions')
add(75,'Meromorphic parametrization needs a fixed divisor',[133],5323,5355,'false as stated','E',
'The meromorphic-function description in4.4b omits a fixed divisor D0 from the expression for members, and partc uses multiplication without consistently retaining that divisor. Effective principal divisors alone do not describe ordinary projective linear systems.',
'The classical formula is D0+div(φ) with φ in a finite-dimensional subspace of H^0(X,O(D0)), up to a common nonzero meromorphic multiplier and corresponding change of D0. The live text must not be silently completed by a deleted D0.',
'Stacks0BXJ,Stacks056P','Proof: on proper integral P^1 every effective principal divisor has degree0, hence is zero. The degree1 hyperplane system has nonzero effective divisors, so it cannot have the printed parametrization solely by effective principal divisors.',
refine='Fix a Cartier divisor D0 and a finite-dimensional space V of meromorphic functions with D0+div(φ) effective for every nonzero φ∈V. Its projective lines parametrize the corresponding linear system. Changing trivialization changes both V and D0.',group='linear_system_questions')
add(76,'A deleted finite-presentation limit step',[134,135],5360,5428,'corrected by himself later','A',
'The letter of29September1965 includes a deleted argument that a constructible good locus which becomes the whole base at a filtered affine limit is eventually the whole base at a finite stage, in the finite-presentation and flat setting under discussion.',
'EGA IV3 §8 limit theorems provide the standard eventual-descent principle. This is a cancelled proof passage, not evidence that the result was abandoned as false. The supplied letter gives no mathematical reason for the deletion.',
'EGAIV3','Proof: a constructible complement in a quasi-compact finite-presentation situation is a finite union of locally closed finite-presentation pieces. If each has empty pullback to the limit, the finite data witnessing emptiness vanish at a sufficiently late stage; take one common stage for the finite union.',group='limits')
add(77,'The isolated Hodge formula',[136],5433,5458,'stated without proof','F',
'The fragment writes H_n(X) as a sum of groups H^q(X,Ω^p) but does not identify H_n or supply hypotheses sufficient to choose between algebraic de Rham cohomology, analytic singular cohomology or another grading.',
'Complex smooth proper Hodge decomposition and algebraic de Rham spectral-sequence degeneration are related but different precise results; Deligne–Illusie1987 gives a characteristic-p liftability theorem, not a canonical splitting of this unqualified formula. The fragment remains unidentifiable at its literal level.',
'DeligneIllusie1987',confidence='low',group='hodge')
